PR #629  overall: potential_risk
Spec: Proprietary ACLIC delivery mode dropped claimi and aliased hart CSRs to platform registers, risking unacknowledgeable interrupts and conflated hart/platform interrupt state — fixed by adopting standard APLIC direct-delivery MMIO subset with proper IDC structure.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:28-32  imsic_mrif (struct)
      Struct faithfully mirrors the AIA-defined Machine-level Registered Interface File layout: per-EIX eip[]/eie[] arrays, eithreshold, and eidelivery. No proprietary fields or aliases. Used as both the persistent SW-file (per-VCPU) and temporary stack-local MRIF for batched updates — clean separation between the two roles.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:23-26  imsic_mrif_eix (struct)
      Sub-struct holds eip[] (pending) and eie[] (enable) as unsigned long bitmask arrays per EIX index, exactly matching the AIA IMSIC register file layout. Each array element covers BITS_PER_TYPE(u64) interrupt identities. No deviation from the standard interface.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:248-281  imsic_mrif_topei
      Implements standard AIA TOPEI discovery: clamps max ID by eithreshold (NOP if eithreshold is zero or exceeds nr_msis), scans eie[] & eip[] atomically via imsic_mrif_atomic_read, and skips bit 0 of EIP0/EIE0 (architecturally reserved). Returns (ID << TOPEI_ID_SHIFT) | ID. This is a pure read — it does NOT claim the interrupt; claiming happens separately via the TOPEI write path in kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_imsic_rmw. Correct per AIA spec where TOPEI read is non-destructive.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:310-363  imsic_mrif_rmw
      MMIO-level RMW handler for all IMSIC ISEL selectors. Uses the custom imsic_mrif_atomic_rmw() (inline LR.W/SC.W loop with .aq/.rl ordering) for all writes — correct fence semantics for multi-hart coherence of the SW-file. Enforces: bit 0 of EIP0/EIE0 is read-only (architecturally reserved), 64-bit only allows even-numbered ISEL indices (aligned 64-bit access), and bounds-checks nr_eix. Properly virtualises the standard IMSIC register interface.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:614-635  imsic_swfile_extirq_update
      The critical bridge from MRIF state to the virtual external interrupt line. Holds imsic->swfile_extirq_lock around the read-check-update sequence, atomically reading eidelivery, calling topei(), and asserting/de-asserting IRQ_VS_EXT. The comment explicitly documents the critical-section rationale: prevents lost interrupts from a read-to-pei / update-pending TOCTOU. Properly synchronized.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:902-943  kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_imsic_rmw
      Guest TOPEI read returns the topei value (non-destructive discovery) via imsic_mrif_topei(). Guest TOPEI write claims the interrupt by clear_bit() on swfile eip[] at the matching index. Non-TOPEI ISEL values delegate to imsic_mrif_rmw() which is fully atomic. After any write (wr_mask non-zero), calls imsic_swfile_extirq_update() which takes the lock to safely re-evaluate IRQ_VS_EXT. The claim mechanism is present and correct — clear_bit is an atomic bitop, and the subsequent extirq_update uses the lock to prevent lost-assertion races.
  [OK] arch/riscv/kvm/aia_imsic.c:1026-1056  kvm_riscv_vcpu_aia_imsic_inject
      Injection entry point: validates guest_index==0 and offset is SETIPNUM_LE/BE, does byte-swap for BE, bounds-checks iid against nr_msis. Under vsfile_lock (read), either writes directly to hardware VS-file via writel() if a VS-file is assigned, or sets the pending bit in swfile and calls imsic_swfile_extirq_update() to propagate to the virtual interrupt line. The vsfile_lock read-side critical section correctly guards the vsfile_cpu/vsfile_va check decision. No aliasing of hart-local to platform-global registers.
